---
import Layout from '../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="Lesson 9.5: Arrays and Data Structures - C00lnerd Tutoring">
    <div style="max-width: 1000px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 2rem;">
        
        <!-- Header -->
        <div style="background: linear-gradient(135deg, #667eea 0%, #764ba2 100%); color: white; padding: 3rem; border-radius: 15px; margin-bottom: 2rem; text-align: center;">
            <h1 style="font-size: 2.5rem; font-weight: bold; margin: 0 0 1rem 0;">Lesson 9.5: Arrays and Data Structures</h1>
            <p style="font-size: 1.2rem; margin: 0 0 1rem 0; opacity: 0.9;">Organizing collections of values so your programs can handle patterns, melodies, and poses</p>
            <div style="background: rgba(255,255,255,0.1); padding: 1rem; border-radius: 8px; margin-top: 1.5rem;">
                <p style="margin: 0; font-weight: 600;">🧩 This supplemental lesson prepares you for Lesson 10, where melodies and motion patterns are stored as arrays and structs.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Why This Lesson -->
        <div style="background: white; padding: 2rem; border-radius: 15px; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); margin-bottom: 2rem;">
            <h2 style="color: #667eea; font-size: 1.8rem; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">🤔 Why This Lesson Exists</h2>
            <p style="color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.7; margin-bottom: 1rem;">
                In Lesson 10, we play melodies and run motion patterns. A melody is <strong>not one number</strong> — it is a
                <em>list</em> of notes played in order. A robot arm pose is not one number either — it is a
                <em>group</em> of angles that belong together (base, elbow, shoulder, gripper).
            </p>
            <p style="color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.7; margin: 0;">
                C++ gives us two tools for this: <strong>arrays</strong> (lists of the same type of value) and
                <strong>structs</strong> (groups of related values that belong to one "thing"). This lesson shows you both
                with small examples you can run on the same hardware you already have wired up.
            </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Learning Objectives -->
        <div style="background: white; padding: 2rem; border-radius: 15px; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); margin-bottom: 2rem;">
            <h2 style="color: #667eea; font-size: 1.8rem; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">🎯 Learning Objectives</h2>
            <div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 2rem;">
                <div>
                    <h3 style="color: #2d3748; font-size: 1.3rem; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1rem;">By the end of this lesson, you will:</h3>
                    <ul style="list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0; color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.8;">
                        <li>• Declare and use arrays to store lists of values</li>
                        <li>• Loop through arrays with <code>for</code> loops</li>
                        <li>• Use parallel arrays for related data (note + duration)</li>
                        <li>• Group related fields with <code>struct</code></li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <h3 style="color: #2d3748; font-size: 1.3rem; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1rem;">Key Concepts:</h3>
                    <ul style="list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0; color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.8;">
                        <li>• Zero-based indexing (<code>myArray[0]</code> is the first item)</li>
                        <li>• <code>sizeof()</code> to find array length safely</li>
                        <li>• Arrays of structs for patterns and poses</li>
                        <li>• How this connects to Lesson 10's melodies and the robot arm</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Section 1: The Problem Arrays Solve -->
        <div style="background: white; padding: 2rem; border-radius: 15px; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); margin-bottom: 2rem;">
            <h2 style="color: #667eea; font-size: 1.8rem; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">📝 Section 1: The Problem Arrays Solve</h2>
            
            <p style="color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.7; margin-bottom: 1rem;">
                Imagine storing a 5-note melody using only single variables:
            </p>

            <div style="position: relative;">
                <button onclick="copyCode(this)" style="position: absolute; top: 8px; right: 8px; background-color: #2563eb; color: white; border: none; padding: 4px 8px; border-radius: 4px; cursor: pointer; font-size: 12px; z-index: 10;">📋 Copy</button>
                <pre style="background: #1a202c; color: #e2e8f0; padding: 1rem; border-radius: 6px; overflow-x: auto; font-family: 'Courier New', monospace; margin: 1rem 0;"><code>{`// Without arrays - painful and repetitive!
int note1 = 262;
int note2 = 294;
int note3 = 330;
int note4 = 349;
int note5 = 392;

// To play them you would copy/paste tone() five times...
// What if the melody had 50 notes?`}</code></pre>
            </div>

            <p style="color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.7; margin: 1rem 0;">
                An <strong>array</strong> stores many values under one name. Each value gets an <strong>index</strong>
                (its position number), starting at <code>0</code>:
            </p>

            <div style="position: relative;">
                <button onclick="copyCode(this)" style="position: absolute; top: 8px; right: 8px; background-color: #2563eb; color: white; border: none; padding: 4px 8px; border-radius: 4px; cursor: pointer; font-size: 12px; z-index: 10;">📋 Copy</button>
                <pre style="background: #1a202c; color: #e2e8f0; padding: 1rem; border-radius: 6px; overflow-x: auto; font-family: 'Courier New', monospace; margin: 1rem 0;"><code>{`// With an array - one name, many values
int notes[] = {262, 294, 330, 349, 392};

// notes[0] is 262 (first item)
// notes[4] is 392 (fifth item - index counts from 0!)`}</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div style="background: #fef3c7; padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 8px; margin-top: 1.5rem; border-left: 4px solid #f59e0b;">
                <p style="color: #92400e; margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
                    ⚠️ <strong>Indexing gotcha:</strong> an array of 5 items has indexes <code>0</code> through <code>4</code>.
                    Asking for <code>notes[5]</code> reads memory outside the array — a classic bug source.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Section 2: Looping Through Arrays -->
        <div style="background: white; padding: 2rem; border-radius: 15px; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); margin-bottom: 2rem;">
            <h2 style="color: #667eea; font-size: 1.8rem; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">🔁 Section 2: Looping Through Arrays</h2>
            
            <p style="color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.7; margin-bottom: 1rem;">
                Arrays shine when combined with a <code>for</code> loop — the loop counter <code>i</code> becomes the index.
                Use <code>sizeof(array) / sizeof(array[0])</code> to get the number of items automatically:
            </p>

            <div style="position: relative;">
                <button onclick="copyCode(this)" style="position: absolute; top: 8px; right: 8px; background-color: #2563eb; color: white; border: none; padding: 4px 8px; border-radius: 4px; cursor: pointer; font-size: 12px; z-index: 10;">📋 Copy</button>
                <pre style="background: #1a202c; color: #e2e8f0; padding: 1rem; border-radius: 6px; overflow-x: auto; font-family: 'Courier New', monospace; margin: 1rem 0;"><code>{`// Array Basics - read and print every item
int notes[] = {262, 294, 330, 349, 392};

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(9600);
  
  int count = sizeof(notes) / sizeof(notes[0]);  // = 5
  
  for (int i = 0; i < count; i++) {
    Serial.print("notes[");
    Serial.print(i);
    Serial.print("] = ");
    Serial.println(notes[i]);
  }
}

void loop() {
  // nothing needed here
}`}</code></pre>
            </div>

            <p style="color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.7; margin-top: 1rem;">
                <strong>Why <code>sizeof</code>?</strong> If you later add a 6th note, the loop still works — you never have to
                update a hard-coded "5" somewhere else in the code. This is a habit worth building now.
            </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Section 3: Parallel Arrays -->
        <div style="background: white; padding: 2rem; border-radius: 15px; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); margin-bottom: 2rem;">
            <h2 style="color: #667eea; font-size: 1.8rem; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">🎵 Section 3: Parallel Arrays (Notes + Durations)</h2>
            
            <p style="color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.7; margin-bottom: 1rem;">
                A melody needs <em>two</em> facts per note: the <strong>frequency</strong> and the <strong>duration</strong>.
                The simplest approach is two arrays kept in the same order — <code>melody[i]</code> pairs with
                <code>noteDurations[i]</code>. This is exactly what Lesson 10's melody code does
                (buzzer on <strong>pin 8</strong>):
            </p>

            <div style="position: relative;">
                <button onclick="copyCode(this)" style="position: absolute; top: 8px; right: 8px; background-color: #2563eb; color: white; border: none; padding: 4px 8px; border-radius: 4px; cursor: pointer; font-size: 12px; z-index: 10;">📋 Copy</button>
                <pre style="background: #1a202c; color: #e2e8f0; padding: 1rem; border-radius: 6px; overflow-x: auto; font-family: 'Courier New', monospace; margin: 1rem 0;"><code>{`// Parallel Arrays Mini-Melody (buzzer on pin 8)
const int buzzerPin = 8;

int melody[]        = {262, 330, 392, 523};  // C4, E4, G4, C5
int noteDurations[] = {4,   4,   4,   2};    // 4 = quarter note, 2 = half note

void setup() {
  int count = sizeof(melody) / sizeof(melody[0]);
  
  for (int i = 0; i < count; i++) {
    int duration = 1000 / noteDurations[i];
    tone(buzzerPin, melody[i], duration);
    delay(duration * 1.3);  // small gap between notes
    noTone(buzzerPin);
  }
}

void loop() {
  // Played once in setup()
}`}</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div style="background: #eff6ff; padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 8px; margin-top: 1.5rem; border-left: 4px solid #3b82f6;">
                <p style="color: #1e40af; margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
                    💡 Parallel arrays work, but they have a weakness: the pairing lives only in your head. If the two lists
                    get out of order, the song is wrong. Structs (next section) fix that by keeping each note's data together.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Section 4: Structs -->
        <div style="background: white; padding: 2rem; border-radius: 15px; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); margin-bottom: 2rem;">
            <h2 style="color: #667eea; font-size: 1.8rem; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">📦 Section 4: Structs — Grouping Related Data</h2>
            
            <p style="color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.7; margin-bottom: 1rem;">
                A <code>struct</code> bundles several fields into one named type. Think of one "note" as a package containing
                a frequency <em>and</em> a duration. For our robot arm, a "pose" will package several servo angles.
            </p>

            <div style="position: relative;">
                <button onclick="copyCode(this)" style="position: absolute; top: 8px; right: 8px; background-color: #2563eb; color: white; border: none; padding: 4px 8px; border-radius: 4px; cursor: pointer; font-size: 12px; z-index: 10;">📋 Copy</button>
                <pre style="background: #1a202c; color: #e2e8f0; padding: 1rem; border-radius: 6px; overflow-x: auto; font-family: 'Courier New', monospace; margin: 1rem 0;"><code>{`// Struct Basics - one package holding related fields
struct Note {
  int frequency;   // Hz
  int duration;    // milliseconds
};

// Create two Note "packages"
Note noteA = {262, 500};   // C4 for half a second
Note noteB = {392, 250};   // G4 for a quarter second

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(9600);
  
  // Access fields with the dot operator
  Serial.print("noteA frequency: ");
  Serial.println(noteA.frequency);
  Serial.print("noteB duration: ");
  Serial.println(noteB.duration);
}

void loop() {
}`}</code></pre>
            </div>

            <h3 style="color: #2d3748; font-size: 1.3rem; font-weight: bold; margin: 1.5rem 0 1rem 0;">Arrays of Structs — the Best of Both</h3>
            <p style="color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.7; margin-bottom: 1rem;">
                Combine them: an <strong>array of structs</strong> gives you a list where every item is a complete package.
                This is the exact pattern Lesson 10 uses for its audio-motion sequences:
            </p>

            <div style="position: relative;">
                <button onclick="copyCode(this)" style="position: absolute; top: 8px; right: 8px; background-color: #2563eb; color: white; border: none; padding: 4px 8px; border-radius: 4px; cursor: pointer; font-size: 12px; z-index: 10;">📋 Copy</button>
                <pre style="background: #1a202c; color: #e2e8f0; padding: 1rem; border-radius: 6px; overflow-x: auto; font-family: 'Courier New', monospace; margin: 1rem 0;"><code>{`// Array of Structs - a list of complete packages
struct Step {
  int servoPos;    // degrees (for pin 9 servo)
  int frequency;   // Hz (for pin 8 buzzer)
  int duration;    // ms
};

Step pattern[] = {
  {0,   262, 400},   // step 0: left + low note
  {90,  330, 400},   // step 1: center + mid note
  {180, 392, 400},   // step 2: right + high note
};

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(9600);
  
  int count = sizeof(pattern) / sizeof(pattern[0]);
  for (int i = 0; i < count; i++) {
    Serial.print("Step ");
    Serial.print(i);
    Serial.print(": servo=");
    Serial.print(pattern[i].servoPos);
    Serial.print(" deg, freq=");
    Serial.print(pattern[i].frequency);
    Serial.println(" Hz");
  }
}

void loop() {
}`}</code></pre>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Hands-On Activity -->
        <div style="background: white; padding: 2rem; border-radius: 15px; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); margin-bottom: 2rem;">
            <h2 style="color: #667eea; font-size: 1.8rem; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">🛠️ Hands-On: Preset Pose Sequencer</h2>
            
            <p style="color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.7; margin-bottom: 1rem;">
                Use your standard wiring (<strong>servo on pin 9</strong>, <strong>buzzer on pin 8</strong>,
                <strong>button on A2</strong>). Each button press advances to the next pose in an array of structs —
                this is the same machinery that will drive "preset poses" on the robot arm:
            </p>

            <div style="position: relative;">
                <button onclick="copyCode(this)" style="position: absolute; top: 8px; right: 8px; background-color: #2563eb; color: white; border: none; padding: 4px 8px; border-radius: 4px; cursor: pointer; font-size: 12px; z-index: 10;">📋 Copy</button>
                <pre style="background: #1a202c; color: #e2e8f0; padding: 1rem; border-radius: 6px; overflow-x: auto; font-family: 'Courier New', monospace; margin: 1rem 0;"><code>{`// Preset Pose Sequencer
// Servo -> pin 9, Buzzer -> pin 8, Button -> A2 (to GND, INPUT_PULLUP)
#include <Servo.h>

Servo joint;
const int servoPin = 9;
const int buzzerPin = 8;
const int buttonPin = A2;

// Each pose = one package of related values
struct Pose {
  int angle;
  int beepFreq;
};

Pose poses[] = {
  {30,  262},    // C4
  {60,  294},    // D4
  {90,  330},    // E4
  {120, 392},    // G4
  {150, 523}     // C5
};

int poseIndex = 0;
int poseCount = sizeof(poses) / sizeof(poses[0]);
int lastButton = HIGH;

void setup() {
  joint.attach(servoPin);
  pinMode(buzzerPin, OUTPUT);
  pinMode(buttonPin, INPUT_PULLUP);
  Serial.begin(9600);
  
  // Start at first pose
  joint.write(poses[0].angle);
  Serial.println("Press button to step through poses");
}

void loop() {
  // read -> decide -> act
  int button = digitalRead(buttonPin);
  
  if (lastButton == HIGH && button == LOW) {
    poseIndex = poseIndex + 1;
    if (poseIndex >= poseCount) {
      poseIndex = 0;  // wrap around to the start
    }
    
    joint.write(poses[poseIndex].angle);
    tone(buzzerPin, poses[poseIndex].beepFreq, 150);
    
    Serial.print("Pose ");
    Serial.print(poseIndex);
    Serial.print(" -> angle ");
    Serial.println(poses[poseIndex].angle);
  }
  
  lastButton = button;
}`}</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div style="background: #e6fffa; padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 8px; margin-top: 1.5rem;">
                <h4 style="color: #065f46; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">Try these extensions:</h4>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 1.25rem; color: #065f46; line-height: 1.8;">
                    <li>• Add a <code>holdTime</code> field to <code>Pose</code> and delay that long at each pose</li>
                    <li>• Use the potentiometer (A0) to choose which pose index to jump to</li>
                    <li>• Make a second array of poses and let the button toggle between arrays</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Quick Check -->
        <div style="background: white; padding: 2rem; border-radius: 15px; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); margin-bottom: 2rem;">
            <h2 style="color: #667eea; font-size: 1.8rem; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">📝 Quick Check</h2>
            <ol style="color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.9; padding-left: 1.5rem; margin: 0;">
                <li>If an array is declared as <code>int data[] = {5, 10, 15, 20};</code>, what is <code>data[2]</code>?</li>
                <li>Why does <code>sizeof(notes) / sizeof(notes[0])</code> give the number of items?</li>
                <li>What is the weakness of parallel arrays that a <code>struct</code> fixes?</li>
                <li>Write a <code>struct</code> called <code>ArmPose</code> with fields for a base angle and an elbow angle.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- Where This Goes Next -->
        <div style="background: #ecfdf5; padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 2rem; border-left: 4px solid #059669;">
            <h4 style="color: #065f46; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">Where this goes next</h4>
            <p style="color: #065f46; margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
                Lesson 10 uses parallel arrays for melodies (<code>melody[]</code> + <code>noteDurations[]</code>) and an
                array of structs (<code>struct Pattern</code>) for coordinated sound + servo patterns. Later, the robot arm's
                "preset poses" (home, ready, reach) will be arrays of structs exactly like the activity above — but with
                one angle per joint.
            </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Navigation -->
        <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center; margin-top: 2rem;">
            <div>
                <a href="/programming/lessons32/lesson9" style="background-color: #6b7280; color: white; padding: 12px 20px; border-radius: 8px; text-decoration: none; font-weight: 600; border: 2px solid #4b5563; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px -1px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1); transition: all 0.2s; display: inline-block;" onmouseover="this.style.backgroundColor='#4b5563'; this.style.transform='translateY(-2px)'; this.style.boxShadow='0 10px 15px -3px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1)'" onmouseout="this.style.backgroundColor='#6b7280'; this.style.transform='translateY(0px)'; this.style.boxShadow='0 4px 6px -1px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1)'">
                    ← Previous: Lesson 9
                </a>
            </div>
            <div>
                <a href="/programming/" style="background-color: #059669; color: white; padding: 12px 20px; border-radius: 8px; text-decoration: none; font-weight: 600; border: 2px solid #047857; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px -1px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1); transition: all 0.2s; display: inline-block;" onmouseover="this.style.backgroundColor='#047857'; this.style.transform='translateY(-2px)'; this.style.boxShadow='0 10px 15px -3px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1)'" onmouseout="this.style.backgroundColor='#059669'; this.style.transform='translateY(0px)'; this.style.boxShadow='0 4px 6px -1px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1)'">
                    Course Overview
                </a>
            </div>
            <div>
                <a href="/programming/lessons32/lesson10" style="background-color: #2563eb; color: white; padding: 12px 20px; border-radius: 8px; text-decoration: none; font-weight: 600; border: 2px solid #1d4ed8; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px -1px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1); transition: all 0.2s; display: inline-block;" onmouseover="this.style.backgroundColor='#1d4ed8'; this.style.transform='translateY(-2px)'; this.style.boxShadow='0 10px 15px -3px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1)'" onmouseout="this.style.backgroundColor='#2563eb'; this.style.transform='translateY(0px)'; this.style.boxShadow='0 4px 6px -1px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1)'">
